@meta
id: 29-01
title: A plugin is a file in a directory
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: packadd
par: 22
minutes: 5
vscode: install an extension, restart, hope it does not eat 400MB
open: order.provider.ts
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
There is a plugin in this lesson's directory. Nothing was downloaded and
you did not install it — the dojo shipped it as one of the lesson's files,
the same way it ships `order.provider.ts`. Look:

    pack/dojo/start/sortimports/plugin/sortimports.vim

Twenty-odd lines of vimscript. Vim read it at startup because of where it
sits, and because it was read, this vim has a command it would not
otherwise have: `:SortImports`. That is all a plugin is. Not a bundle, not
a marketplace listing, not a background process. A file of vimscript in a
directory vim knows to look in.

The import block at the top of `order.provider.ts` has drifted out of
order. Sort it, using the command the plugin gave you. Read the plugin
first if you want to know what it is going to do — it sorts on the module
path, the text after `from `.

@keys
| `:SortImports` | the command this lesson's plugin defines |
| `:command` | list every command defined, plugins included |
| `:scriptnames` | every file vim has sourced, in the order it sourced them |

@hints
The command is already there. You do not have to load anything, require
anything or restart. Type a colon and the name.
--
`:SortImports` then Enter. If you want to see where it came from first,
`:scriptnames` lists every file vim sourced and the plugin will be near
the bottom.

@solution
    :SortImports<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Seventeen keystrokes, and the interesting part is the ones you did not
have to press.

The directory layout is the whole mechanism, so it is worth reading once,
slowly:

    pack / dojo / start / sortimports / plugin / sortimports.vim
      |      |      |         |           |
      |      |      |         |           `-- sourced at startup
      |      |      |         `-- one plugin
      |      |      `-- "start" means load it automatically
      |      `-- a group name you choose; it means nothing to vim
      `-- a directory named pack/ on the 'packpath'

Your own plugins go in `~/.vim/pack/<anything>/start/<plugin>/`, because
`~/.vim` is on the default `packpath`. This lesson cheats by one option —
`set packpath^=.` adds the current directory too, which is why a package
sitting next to your files works at all. Run `:set packpath?` and you will
see it.

This is vim's built-in package system. It landed in vim 8, in 2016, and
from that day onward installing a plugin has been `git clone` into a
directory. Chapter 29 spends four lessons on the machinery and then twelve
on the three plugins that actually change your day.

@start pack/dojo/start/sortimports/plugin/sortimports.vim
" dojo-sortimports.vim - sort an ES module import block by module path.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_sortimports')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_sortimports = 1

function! s:sort_imports() abort
  let first = 0
  let last = 0
  for n in range(1, line('$'))
    if getline(n) =~# '^import '
      let first = first == 0 ? n : first
      let last = n
    endif
  endfor
  if first == 0
    echo 'SortImports: no import block found'
    return
  endif
  " :sort /pat/ sorts on what comes AFTER the match, so this sorts on the
  " module path rather than on the names being imported.
  execute first . ',' . last . 'sort /from /'
endfunction

command! SortImports call s:sort_imports()

@start order.provider.ts
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
